 17592186045417] [17592186097562 #edb/ref 1061 19014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097562 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "afa24342-fd53-5361-8f24-8c57d1a13102" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097562 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097563 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097563 #edb/ref 1061 19024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097563 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cbf661ea-7d87-5c8d-8bee-63a814c037dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097563 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097832 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ shown in the picture below. The vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$ and a length (magnitude) that is twice the length of $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n|2\\mathbf{a}|=2 \\cdot |\\mathbf{a}|\n$$\n\n![](images/01/01c393b2df3d39e4f62faca475a26ea835e1ab88266628509d58e21edd36c79b.png)\n\nOn the other hand, $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$ and its magnitude is $1.5$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$: \n\n$$\n|-1.5\\mathbf{a}|=1.5 \\cdot |\\mathbf{a}|\n$$\n\n![](images/73/73ea353af74ab9518ea7a963985ad26ad36723c7e3a335391628f1eba208139e.png)\n\nNotice that $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ are both parallel to $\\mathbf a,$ and are parallel to each other." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097832 #edb/ref 1065 2905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097832 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "977eec28-841a-556b-a6c4-937a625db1b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097832 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097834 #edb/ref 1064 "For any vector $\\mathbf{a}$ the vector $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ is equal to the zero vector: \n\n$$\n0\\cdot\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to *any* vector." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097834 #edb/ref 1065 2907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097834 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d5934891-d63d-5d05-be3e-b864a8081187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097834 #edb/ref 1067 "The Zero Vector is Parallel to Any Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097852 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are called **parallel** (or **collinear**) if there exists a number $\\lambda$ such that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSometimes, we'll use a special notation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\parallel \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nto denote that $\\mathbf{a}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097852 #edb/ref 1065 3401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097852 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9fc7b4c2-c418-5980-88be-ea7609a45ba0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097852 #edb/ref 1067 "Determining Whether Two Vectors Are Parallel" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503923 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503927 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503923 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503923 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/55/55ac7d0e79f21610191ebb3c861bb1a5d8e3eaf3ad625ad121e7f815bdc3c517.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $a$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $-\\frac{3}{2}a$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503923 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ad91052-6847-54a3-94f7-e0dcea362c59" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503923 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{3}{2}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5ae3d645aaa0a5cae7a8c5ee0b346310591eaf2bc884475c930ceff3f5bdb68.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503928 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503931 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/96/9668d08ce01afb28e2aa7613eed2ffc37995f5108e0de1a515ad02705c1ae844.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $2a$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}a$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "199672e2-40b9-55f2-9720-3f06a31d2dd8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503931 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that both $2a$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nNext, for the vector $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{3}{2}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/7f/7f63c606fff90d3d867a8b24a50855cc66c0fbb9e97748f429f5a53641f29c67.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503937 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503919 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503937 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503937 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cc/cc3f84b6d1a75844727d07e0fddc1f7cccd0aec76fb150022d0d62123b1bb553.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $2a$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}a$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503937 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "253ac3d4-9991-5df9-ac19-bf2e42dd789a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503937 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that both $2a$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nNext, for the vector $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/bc/bcbc4dbb8e224b898bc8f521a739614e88348d75fbea1f372f039d4c7d3682a5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503940 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503936 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503940 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503940 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cb/cb9a3adfca909938806eff56b7e7eff2c52e40d0884b9ae1213e12d3f33e7029.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $2a$ and $-\\frac{2}{3}a$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503940 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2aeb052d-461d-5aa2-913f-bd8dca640f26" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503940 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that both $2a$ and $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nNext, for the vector $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{2}{3}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f435d3754370fe01575b6d8e37aec6a9e7bf3b5ecd432f68a878264f896023ba.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503984 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503942 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503942 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/55/55ac7d0e79f21610191ebb3c861bb1a5d8e3eaf3ad625ad121e7f815bdc3c517.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $a$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $-\\frac{2}{3}a$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186503942 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2cfcd0b2-d6f3-5e5d-b816-